{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790487761,"nonce":"WR28Pnzsp645zA61UGuNHnjuEeY=","title":"Compiler: the error-flag and bounds-versioning passes add compile time to every Python program","message":"NUC, chaos (ZYNTAX_TRACE_OPT_PHASES=1, 3 runs of -n 3): optimiser total 126.8 ms at 1faeba4d, 134.6 ms at d11c6510 with the error-flag pass off, of which bounds_version (cbbca5f6) is 8.6 ms; the error-flag pass itself (d11c6510) adds 9.1-9.3 ms over about 115 calls. Whole-process wall on chaos: 1faeba4d about 550 ms, d11c6510 about 569 with the pass, 558 without. A dense memoised solve only cut error_flag to 8.5 ms, so the time is in the scans, the CFG edge rebuild, threading's block clone plus DominatorTree check, and folding's whole-function substitution. Candidates: an undo log and a local dominance check instead of cloning, uses_escape_past once per arm, folding that rewrites only the compare and branch, running the pass only where a raiser was inlined; for bounds_version, skip functions with no counted loop over a list header before any analysis. Within the A/B spread today, but it is a per-program cost.","files":null}]}